# Deploy — AWS ECS Fargate

The `iac/` directory holds Terraform for a complete Airflow deployment on
AWS, driven entirely through `dude iac …`. Architecture:

```mermaid
graph LR
  U[Browser / API clients] --> ALB
  ALB --> WEB[web service<br/>api-server]
  CORE[core service<br/>scheduler · dag-processor · triggerer] -->|RunTask| W1[worker container<br/>one per Airflow task]
  WEB --- RDS[(RDS Postgres<br/>metadata DB)]
  CORE --- RDS
  W1 --- RDS
  W1 -->|task logs| S3[(S3 logs bucket)]
  WEB -->|reads logs| S3
```

- **One image, everything baked in** — `dude iac ship` builds
  `airflow/Dockerfile.prod` (requirements + dags + plugins +
  webserver_config), pushes it to the shared ECR repo and rolls every task
  definition to the new tag. A deploy is atomic and reproducible; there is no
  live DAG mount in AWS.
- **ECS executor: every task in a dedicated container.** The scheduler runs
  the AWS ECS executor, which calls `RunTask` against the `worker` task
  definition for each Airflow task instance. Sizing per task container is
  `worker_cpu` / `worker_memory` in terraform.tfvars. Because workers are
  ephemeral, task logs go to S3 (remote logging) and the UI reads them back
  from there.
- **Secrets** — Terraform owns the *core* secret (DB URI, Fernet key, JWT
  secret{{#unless withEntraId}}, bootstrap admin password{{/unless}}); you own the *app* secret via
  `dude iac secrets`. Keys listed in `app_secret_keys` become env vars in
  every container. Plain config goes in `app_environment`.

## First deploy

```bash
dude iac login --env dev
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <your-org> --env dev --yes
dude iac init --env dev
dude iac apply --env dev          # VPC, ALB, RDS, ECS, secrets (~10 min)
{{#if withEntraId}}
dude iac secrets --env dev --set AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>
# Entra: add https://$(dude iac output --env dev …airflow_url)/auth/oauth-authorized/azure
# to the app registration's redirect URIs.
{{/if}}
dude iac migrate --env dev        # metadata schema{{#unless withEntraId}} + admin user{{/unless}}
dude iac ship --env dev           # build + push + deploy the image
dude iac output --env dev         # airflow_url, db endpoint, …
```

{{#unless withEntraId}}
Sign in as `admin`; the generated password lives in the core secret:

```bash
aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
  --secret-id {{projectName}}/dev/core --query SecretString --output text | jq -r .admin_password
```

{{/unless}}
## The example DAGs on AWS

- `example_ecs_task` — works out of the box: the IaC injects `ECS_CLUSTER`,
  `ECS_WORKER_TASK_DEFINITION`, `ECS_SUBNETS`, `ECS_SECURITY_GROUPS` into
  every container, so the DAG's guard passes and `EcsRunTaskOperator` launches
  a dedicated container on the same cluster.
- `example_batch_compute` — simulates by default. To run the real path,
  provision an AWS Batch job queue + job definition (not part of this
  Terraform on purpose — Batch compute environments are very workload-
  specific), then wire them as plain env vars and grant the task role
  `batch:SubmitJob`/`DescribeJobs`:

  ```hcl
  # terraform.tfvars
  app_environment = {
    BATCH_JOB_QUEUE      = "my-queue"
    BATCH_JOB_DEFINITION = "my-jobdef"
  }
  ```

  and trigger with `{"simulate": false}`.
- `example_kubernetes_pod` — add a `kubernetes_default` connection pointing
  at your EKS/other cluster (Admin → Connections) and make sure the task role
  can reach it.

## Scaling & production checklist

- `worker_cpu/memory` — per-task sizing; `web_desired_count` — UI replicas.
- RDS: set `db_multi_az = true`, `db_deletion_protection = true`,
  `db_skip_final_snapshot = false` in prod tfvars.
- Restrict `allowed_cidrs`; set `domain_name`/`route53_zone_name` for HTTPS.
- New env: `dude iac new-env --env prod --from dev`, then bootstrap is shared
  — just `init`, `apply`, `migrate`, `ship`.

## Teardown

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

Removes the env (ECS services, ALB, RDS, secrets, buckets). The shared
backend (state bucket, lock table, ECR) is torn down only when no other
environment still uses it — pass `--keep-backend` to always preserve it.
